shmat() return value : 

	Success : return the address of the attached shared memory segment
	Failure : -1 is returned

shmdt() return value : 

	Success : return 0
	Failure : -1 and errno 

Flags 
-----

	1. SHM_RND 

		The attach occurs at the address : 

			* equal to shmaddr
			* rounded down to the nearest multiple of SHMLBA
		
		Used for address alignment 

	2. SHM_RDONLY 

		* Attaches the shared memory segment in read-only mode 
		* The process must have read permission 
		* If not specified 
			
			* Segment is attached in read-write mode 
			* Process must have read and write permissions 

	3. SHM_REMAP 

		* Indicates that the mapping of the shared memory segment should: 

			* Replace any existing mapping 
			* In the address range starting at shmaddr 
			* Continuing for the size of the segment 

	

	when shmat() is called 

		shm_atime  :- set to current time 
		shm_lpid   :- set to the calling PID 
		shm_nattch :- incremented by 1

	when shmdt() is called 

		shm_dtime  :- set to current time 
		shm_lpid   :- set to the calling PID 
		shm_nattch :- decremented by 1

	
	Disadvantages of shared Memory 

		Synchronization Problem 

			If both process access shared memory at the same time 
			Data may get corrupted 
			Sychronization mechanisms (semaphores, mutexes) are required 

		shmget() Note 
			
			A new shared memory segment is created
			Actual size allocated is 
	
				* Equal to requested size
				* Rounded up to the narest multiple of PAGESIZE 



	Data is shared not because it is a file 
	
	Data is shared because both processes attach to the same shared memory segement 


















		  
			



































